From d5d4608f029b6d9be575244f8945065ce9be6a0e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 15:51:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(players): interpolate negotiated remote movement as
 native floats

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5, protocol 2225, retains three float position
steps across admitted normal movement beyond Java's 64-block cutoff.
Its small-move X samples are 2.166666746, 1.833333373 and 1.5. Java
instead advances through 2.166666657, 1.75 and 1.5. Retain the native
eye origin and float arithmetic inside the entity interpolation phase.

Decorate the real host handler only for remote players. Pair negotiated
receipts with exact Java packets, UUIDs and native lifetimes. Keep the
position, main-rotation and head counters independent. Preserve forced
target completion and queued handoff. Retain eye coordinates between
steps instead of rounding them through Java's feet origin every tick.

A head-only packet applies raw pitch immediately and preserves live yaw,
pending position and body targets, and forced queues. Its head target
uses the maximum remaining position/body counter. Pair the rotation-only
Java packet explicitly so the host path cannot cancel pending movement.
Cancel the superseded Java head lerp through its existing public API.

Apply respawn and teleport positions immediately. Both clear motion and
active interpolation counters while preserving the current head angle
and any queued target. Respawn resets pitch to zero and retains previous
position; teleport retains wrapped pitch and resets previous position.
Retain the native float feet-to-eye roundtrip on teleport. Queue handoff
still runs after a reset leaves all counters at zero.

Native packet dispatch derives motion from raw source position minus
previous StateVector, including head-only packets. Retain exact native
eye history after native body control instead of rounding it through
Java feet coordinates. Body control reads current-minus-previous before
the StateVector copy. A respawn must retain that displacement through
the first body tick. Copy it afterward, including while interpolation is
idle. Historical physics replay must not advance this live history.
Preserve source positions separately from their exact Java targets.

Return known riding, local authority and unpaired packets to the host
path. Normal interpolation retains the existing Java ticking guard;
resets and head-only updates have no native normal-mode readiness gate.
Preserve ordinary Java history. Retire native state on connection, world,
entity, lifetime or mount changes. Keep body formulas and the independent
absolute/delta receive cache and collision-history ownership intact.

Current native helper 0x1402d92c0, callback 0x143630d70, angle helper
0x143631440, target producer 0x1422c8d20 and queue kernel 0x143634ae0
establish these component rules. Instruction controls exclude component
allocation and native scheduler admission. Live comparisons establish
selected steps and poses; complete timing and trajectory parity remain
open alongside mounted and unknown-factory behavior. Current native
packet handler 0x146bd39a0, teleport setter 0x140baed10 and reset method
0x1402c77b0 establish the new packet/reset rules. Matching native/direct
controls distinguish respawn pitch, previous position, retained head
state and queued movement after both resets.

Read-only remote actor vtables identify current PlayerBodyControl::tick
0x1443419f0 through BodyControlComponent and callback 0x146947130. A
respawn moves the body from 40 to 62.5 degrees before the previous-position
copy, then settles at 60.5. A head-only packet received with one position
step left derives motion from the last completed body tick position.
These controls establish history ordering, not scheduler timing.

Publish native remote pitch directly to the rotation field. Java 26.3
Entity.setXRot clamps to +/-90 degrees, while the Bedrock 1.26.60.5
original interpolation callback (143630d70, executable SHA-256
988f6a61403b4381aa41e6c66d64cce441b14e561a9b10f647c2758ef3f2672a)
retains wrapped pitch beyond those bounds. Installed protocol-2225 controls
reproduced +179/-179 packets settling at +90/-90 before this correction.
Keep the direct field access inside the admitted native remote handler;
ordinary Java setters and local look input retain their host behavior.

Retain native previous pitch before LivingEntity normalizes its render
history by360 degrees. Capture the value after interpolation and before
body-tick completion, and use it for immediate head-only updates as well
as render queries. Retire this history with its native context; historical
physics replay cannot advance it.

A read-only live Bedrock1.26.60.5 trace records12 interpolation transitions
and their rotation components on matching protocol2225 peer packets.
Java changed previous179.66669 to-180.33331 at a normal wrap, producing
query errors of360 degrees in11direct and12proxy samples. The native
query retains its unwrapped result. Native executable SHA-256 remains
988f6a61403b4381aa41e6c66d64cce441b14e561a9b10f647c2758ef3f2672a.
Debugger pauses exclude wall-clock timing from this comparison.
